  issn = {0003-4851},
  urldate = {2018-03-11},
  abstract = {The mixed model in a 2-way analysis of variance is characterized by a fixed classification, e.g., treatments, and a random classification, e.g., plots or individuals. If we consider \$k\$ different treatments each applied to everyone of \$n\$ individuals, and assume the usual analysis of variance assumptions of uncorrelated errors, equal variances and normality, an appropriate analysis for the set of \$nk\$ observations \$x\_\textbraceleft ij\textbraceright, i = 1, 2, \textbackslash cdots n, j = 1, 2, \textbackslash cdots k\$, is ???? where the \$F\$ ratio under the null hypothesis has the \$F\$ distribution with \$(k - 1)\$ and \$(k - 1)(n - 1)\$ degrees of freedom. As is well known, if we extend the situation so that the errors have equal correlations instead of being uncorrelated, the \$F\$ ratio has the same distribution. Under the null hypothesis, the numerator estimates the same quantity as the denominator, namely, \$(1 - \textbackslash rho)\textbackslash sigma\textasciicircum 2\$, where \$\textbackslash rho\$ is the constant correlation coefficient among the treatments. This case can also be considered as a sampling of \$n\$ vectors (individuals) from a \$k\$-variate normal population with variance-covariance matrix \$\$V = \textbackslash sigma\textasciicircum 2 \textbackslash begin\textbraceleft pmatrix\textbraceright{} 1 \& \textbackslash rho \& \textbackslash cdots \& \textbackslash rho \textbackslash\textbackslash{} \textbackslash rho \& \& \& \textbackslash vdots \textbackslash\textbackslash{} \textbackslash vdots \& \& \& \textbackslash rho \textbackslash\textbackslash{} \textbackslash rho \& \textbackslash cdots \& \textbackslash rho \& 1\textbackslash end\textbraceleft pmatrix\textbraceright.\$\$ If we consider this type of formulation and suppose the \$k\$ treatment errors to have a multivariate normal distribution with unknown variance-covariance matrix (the same for each individual), then the usual test described above is valid for \$k = 2\$. For \$k {$>$} 2\$, and \$n \textbackslash geqq k\$, Hotelling's \$T\textasciicircum 2\$ is the appropriate test for the homogeneity of the treatment means. However, the working statistician is sometimes confronted with the case where \$k {$>$} n\$, or he does not have the adequate means for computing large order inverse matrices and would therefore like to use the original test ratio which in general does not have the requisite \$F\$ distribution. Box [1] and [2] has given an approximate distribution of the test ratio to be \$F\textbackslash lbrack(k - 1)\textbackslash epsilon, (k - 1)(n - 1)\textbackslash epsilon\textbackslash rbrack\$ where \$\textbackslash epsilon\$ is a function of the population variances and covariances and may further be approximated by the sample variances and covariances. We show in Section 3 that \$\textbackslash epsilon \textbackslash geqq (k - 1)\textasciicircum\textbraceleft -1\textbraceright\$, and therefore a conservative test would be \$F(1, n - 1)\$. Box referred only to one group of \$n\$ individuals. We shall extend his results to a frequently occurring case, namely, the analysis of \$g\$ groups where the \$\textbackslash alpha\$th group has \$n\_\textbackslash alpha\$ individuals, \$\textbackslash alpha = 1, 2, \textbackslash cdots g\$, and \$\textbackslash Sigma\textasciicircum g\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash alpha = 1\textbraceright{} n\_\textbackslash alpha = N\$. We will show that the treatment mean square and the treatment \$\textbackslash times\$ group interaction can be tested in the same approximate fashion by using the Box procedure.},
